---
/**
 * foodcraft / ServicesBN — "Panday Coffee Works": THE PRICE LIST.
 *
 * ROLE. The design's SERVICES element (id="menu"), reproduced down to the
 * number — with two departures, each stated in full below and nowhere else: the
 * rail's noun, and the column-header row. The spine's rail
 * ("02 / Menu") beside a padded body holding a SPLIT HEAD — display h2 hard
 * left, one narrow paragraph baseline-aligned hard right, wrapping under it
 * when there is no room — over a RULED LEDGER. The ledger opens on a full-ink
 * rule and every entry is one five-cell grid row —
 * `minmax(0,48px) minmax(0,1.5fr) minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,1.2fr) minmax(0,90px)`
 * at a 20px gutter, 17px of vertical padding, baseline-aligned, closed by a
 * grey hairline — carrying a mono rust index, the item name in Archivo 700, a
 * tracked mono category, the description, and the price hard right. There is no
 * card, no plate, no photograph and no border anywhere but those two rules:
 * this section is a document, not a grid of tiles.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every number here is lifted from
 * "Panday Coffee Works Landing.dc.html" and none is re-derived: the 52px/34px
 * body pad, the 26px head gap and its 30px bottom margin, the h2's
 * clamp(28px,3.6vw,50px), the 14.5px/1.7 head paragraph over 34ch, the ledger's
 * five tracks and 20px gutter, the 17px row pad, the 11px index, the 17.5px/
 * -.01em name, the 11.5px/.04em uppercase category, the 14px/1.6 description
 * and the 17px price. The five colours the design paints in this section each
 * already have a token of the SAME value, so no literal hex appears below
 * (R1): #17181A --ink (h2, name, price) and, as the ledger's opening rule,
 * --rule-strong · #3C3E42 --ink-2 (head paragraph, description) · #5A5C60
 * --muted (the category) · #A8351B --accent (the index numeral, and the rail
 * label the wrapper paints) · #C8C7C2 --line (the row hairline).
 *
 * CLASS NAMES ARE THIS TEMPLATE'S OWN (`.pcwm-*` — Panday Coffee Works, menu),
 * in step with the rest of the BN family's per-template prefixes: pcw-ab-,
 * pcw-ar-, pcw-vis-, pcw-strip-, pcwq-, pdcr-, pdw-, pdend-, pd-ar-, hbn-,
 * pcbn-. An earlier draft of this file used a bare `.svc-*`, which is distinct
 * to nothing: `.svc-head` and `.svc-price` appear verbatim in
 * foodcraft/services/ServicesAL.astro, `.svc-note` and `.svc-row` in
 * filipino/services/ServicesBB.astro. Astro scopes every rule here, so no page
 * was rendering wrong — but a grep for one of this section's classes has to land
 * on ONE template, and an audit in this family already had to rename a whole set
 * of classes that had been copied verbatim from a sibling. Nothing is inherited
 * from the BM menu but the shape of this comment and its guard discipline; BM's
 * `.mnu-*` describes a photo card this section does not have.
 *
 * THE RAIL SAYS "02 / MENU"; THE DESIGN'S RAIL SAYS "02 / PRICE LIST", and that
 * is a stated deviation rather than a transcription slip. The NUMBER is the
 * design's own, and it indexes the RAILS rather than the render order — Hero 00,
 * About 01, Services 02 — because Header, Trust (full-bleed) and Footer carry no
 * rail index, which is why "02" sits on the FIFTH component PageBN renders. The
 * noun is the family's generic one, because the rail label is a string this FILE
 * derives rather than one the owner typed — the single sanctioned exception to
 * R2 in this template — so it has to read on whatever business lands here, and
 * it is the one visible word tying the spine to the id="menu" that the header
 * and footer link at. Three siblings genericise the same way: AboutBN prints
 * "01 / About" for the design's "01 / The works", GalleryBN "04 / Gallery" for
 * "04 / Plates", AreaBN "09 / Area" for "09 / Dispatch".
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — every path is DECLARED in components/editor/
 * genericContentSchema.ts's `services` group, so none is a dead hook:
 *   services.headline               — the h2, set:html so an owner's own <em>
 *                                     lands where THEY put it in THEIR sentence
 *   services.sub                    — the head's right-hand paragraph
 *   services.items.${i}.title       — the item name (schema: "Title")
 *   services.items.${i}.tag         — the tracked mono category cell
 *                                     (schema: "Badge (floor / area / category)")
 *   services.items.${i}.desc        — the description cell (schema: "Description")
 *   services.items.${i}.price       — the price, hard right (schema: "Price")
 *
 * WHY `tag` AND NOT `meta` FOR THE THIRD COLUMN, since both are free text and a
 * reader will wonder: the design draws that cell as tracked uppercase mono
 * BESIDE the name — a category, which is what the schema's `tag` is labelled
 * ("Badge (floor / area / category)"). `meta` is labelled "Small line ABOVE the
 * title", so an owner filling it would be typing into a position this section
 * does not draw.
 *
 * DERIVED — exactly one thing: the row index ("01", "02", …). It is computed
 * from the row's DISPLAY position (R6), zero-padded to two digits, and it is
 * aria-hidden. It is never stored, never an editable hook, and it renumbers
 * when a blank row is dropped, which is the point: a printed ledger counts the
 * lines it prints. The price is NOT derived — it is printed exactly as typed,
 * never converted, never given a currency symbol this file chose, never totalled
 * and never compared against another row.
 *
 * NOTHING IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE (R2). The design's seven demo drinks are the
 * DESIGNER filling their own template; a drink nobody sells is an invented
 * drink and a price nobody quoted is an invented price, so not one of them is a
 * fallback. Every guard sits OUTSIDE its element, so an empty value removes the
 * ELEMENT — and where a whole COLUMN is empty across every row, the TRACK goes
 * with it (see THE LEDGER'S COLUMNS below), because a cell held open by an
 * empty span is the same fabrication with the text deleted.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, DELIBERATELY:
 *   · THE COLUMN-HEADER ROW. The design prints "Ref · Drink · Origin · Tasting
 *     notes · Price" above the first entry, and those five words are the
 *     DESIGNER describing their own coffee bar. No schema path owns them, so
 *     rendering them would mean this file writing visible copy (R2) — and
 *     "Origin / Tasting notes" printed over a barber's service list is a claim
 *     about what those columns contain that the owner never made. The ledger's
 *     full-ink top rule is kept, so the block still OPENS like a table; only
 *     the row of labels is gone.
 *   · services.tag, the eyebrow. This design moved the eyebrow onto the SPINE:
 *     the rail's rotated "02 / Menu" is this section's index label, and every
 *     ruled section in the design draws its head as h2-plus-paragraph with no
 *     .kick above it. Rendering services.tag here would print a second eyebrow
 *     two centimetres from the first. derive-content-defaults fills it on every
 *     site, so this is a deliberate omission and not a missed hook.
 *   · services.ctaLabel / services.ctaHref. The design's price list carries no
 *     button; the page's actions live in the header, the hero, the procedure
 *     block and the closing band. Emitting the pair here would put a live
 *     sidebar input on a control this section does not draw.
 *   · NO onPage() ANCHOR-REMAP HELPER (R7), for that same reason: this section
 *     emits no href at all and declares no data-href-field, so a helper here
 *     would be dead code, and inventing a button to give it a job is exactly the
 *     trade this family forbids. The section's own id="menu" IS one of the
 *     page's anchors and is what the header and footer link at. If a link is
 *     ever added here, copy onPage() from a sibling with
 *       PAGE_ANCHORS = {menu, about, gallery, visit, faq, main}
 *       ANCHOR_ALIAS = {location→visit, contact→visit, services→menu,
 *                       rooms→menu, host→about, why→about, work→gallery,
 *                       book→main}
 *     and pass tel: / mailto: / http(s): through untouched.
 *   · NO RATING AND NO STAR ROW. The design prints none, so this section makes
 *     no rating claim. (Family rule: N stars are drawn only from a numeric
 *     rating field, clamped 1–5, aria-hidden, with the number itself in the
 *     accessible name — five stars over a row that carried no rating is a
 *     fabricated claim.) services.items[].rating is read by the templates that
 *     draw one; here it would be a hook on nothing.
 *   · services.items[].note, .meta, .duration, .features, .image and the whole
 *     services.whole.* band. The design's row has five cells and no sixth. In
 *     particular .duration (the schema's "Price unit") is NOT appended to the
 *     price: the design draws one price span, and hanging a unit off it would be
 *     this file inventing layout. A row that holds ONLY fields from this list is
 *     dropped rather than printed as a bare numeral (see THE ROW FILTER).
 *   · No image, therefore no .plate and no role="img" anywhere in this file.
 *   · The item name is a <span>, not an <h3>. The design draws a ledger cell in
 *     mixed case at 700, and the wrapper's global heading rule would repaint any
 *     h3 as Archivo 800 UPPERCASE at -.03em — i.e. a heading here would have to
 *     fight four declarations to look like the design. The rows are marked up as
 *     a list instead (ul/li with role="list", which keeps the list semantics
 *     that `list-style:none` strips in some screen readers), so the entries are
 *     still announced as a countable set.
 *
 * THE ROW FILTER. A row counts as real when it carries a title, a category, a
 * description OR a price — the four things this row can DRAW. All four count on
 * purpose: each is a live sidebar input, so dropping a row that holds only a
 * price, or only a description, would silently delete an owner's work. A row
 * holding none of them is an unfilled "+ Add" row and would print as an index
 * numeral over four empty cells.
 *
 * THE LEDGER'S COLUMNS ARE COMPUTED, and this is the one decision most likely
 * to be read as a deviation from the design. The demo fills all five columns;
 * a real site very often fills only name + description + price, because
 * `tag` is optional on every row. Keeping a dead `minmax(0,1fr)` track for a
 * category nobody typed would spend a fifth of the row on nothing and crush the
 * description beside it — and the only way to make that track look intentional
 * is to put an empty span in it, which R2 forbids. So the track list and each
 * cell's column index are computed once in the frontmatter, passed to the
 * single scoped rule as CSS custom properties on the ledger element, and the
 * cells are placed EXPLICITLY rather than auto-flowed — auto-placement would
 * slide a description into the category's column on any row that omitted one.
 * The inline style attribute carries custom properties only: no colour, no
 * size, no rule (R3).
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5). Read out of lib/derive-content-defaults.ts rather than
 * assumed: it fills services.tag and services.headline on every site, and
 * services.sub on every site that has a business name (it derives the sentence
 * from the name), while leaving services.items at []. So a gate that counted the
 * headline would ship a 50px "Services we provide" — that literal default — over
 * a ledger with no lines, on every site that listed nothing. The gate counts
 * BODY content only: at least one real row. There is no photograph or panel in this section to
 * count alongside them.
 *
 * DATA SHAPES. content.services arrives as the wrapper {tag, headline, sub,
 * items[]} OR as a bare legacy array (see lib/services-shape.ts), and a wrapped
 * row may still carry the legacy key names title→name / desc→description that
 * the editor's brief draft-normalisation window wrote. All three are read here:
 * without that mapping every row on such a site fails the filter above and the
 * WHOLE PRICE LIST vanishes while the data sits untouched in the record. A bare
 * string row (the oldest shape) becomes its own title rather than being spread
 * into character keys and lost.
 *
 * ACCESSIBILITY (R9). The rail's "02 / Menu" and each row's index numeral are
 * decorative strings this file derived, so both are aria-hidden — each with ONE
 * literal attribute and no conditional twin beside it. Nothing here is
 * announced that an owner did not type.
 *
 * CONTRAST, on this design's native palette (WCAG 2.x, sRGB relative
 * luminance). Every figure is READ OFF the family's checked BN ratio table for
 * the hexes PageBN declares, not recomputed by hand here — hand arithmetic is
 * what put three earlier audits in this family at odds over the same pair.
 * The four pairs this section actually paints:
 *   --ink   #17181A on --paper #EFEEEA = 15.30:1  (h2, item name, price)
 *   --ink-2 #3C3E42 on --paper          =  9.23:1  (head paragraph, description)
 *   --muted #5A5C60 on --paper          =  5.77:1  (the 11.5px category)
 *   --accent #A8351B on --paper         =  5.67:1  (the 11px index numeral)
 * The tightest pair here is 5.67 at 11px, i.e. normal text needing 4.5, so
 * nothing in this section is below AA.
 *   AND THE WRAPPER AGREES WHERE THE TWO OVERLAP. PageBN's header table carries
 *   three of these four — muted/paper 5.77, accent/paper 5.67, ink/paper 15.30 —
 *   and each is the checked table's value; the 9.23 ink-2 pair is this section's
 *   alone and the wrapper does not list it. The wrapper's `.plate .cap` comment
 *   reads 15.30:1 for --on-ink on --ink, which is the ink/paper pair inverted
 *   (--on-ink is the same #EFEEEA as --paper) and is likewise right. Nothing at
 *   the wrapper is outstanding from here, and this section draws no .cap in any
 *   case; the rest of PageBN's table covers pairs this file never paints and is
 *   the wrapper's to maintain.
 *   SCHEME CAVEAT — every figure above is NATIVE ONLY, because the GROUND
 *   moves too. genericThemeOverrides.ts's buildOverrideCss writes --paper,
 *   --ink, --ink-soft, --muted and --accent with !important on any scheme
 *   pick, so at least one end of all four pairs is repainted. --ink-2 is one
 *   of the tokens PageBN lists as invisible to the engine, which pins that END
 *   of the 9.23 pair — but not the --paper under it, so 9.23 is no more
 *   scheme-proof than the other three.
 *
 * RESPONSIVE (R8). The wrapper owns the shared 760/520 pass and the whole
 * spine — the rail going horizontal, the .sec grid collapsing to one column.
 * This file owns its OWN grid: the five-track ledger becomes THREE tracks at
 * 900px (index | name | price on the first line, category and description
 * spanning beneath), because below that a 1.2fr description cell inside a 20px
 * gutter has less measure than the design ever gave it. Every track is
 * minmax(0,…) and every owner-typed string breaks anywhere, so at 360px nothing
 * can push the page sideways; an unspaced price and a one-word category typed in
 * caps are the two likeliest strings here to arrive unbreakable. There is no
 * button in this section, so the full-width-button rule has nothing to bite on.
 * CLAMPS ARE MONOTONIC ACROSS BREAKPOINTS — traced, not assumed:
 *   · .pcwm-h2 states clamp(28px,3.6vw,50px) ONCE, at base, and is never
 *     restated in a media query. Because this file's scoped rule is (0,2,0) and
 *     the wrapper's is (0,1,1), it also wins INSIDE the wrapper's 760 query, so
 *     the headline does NOT pick up the wrapper's clamp(26px,7.4vw,40px) there.
 *     That is deliberate: 3.6vw is already at its 28px floor by 778px while
 *     7.4vw is still pinned to its 40px cap until 541px, so inheriting the
 *     wrapper's mobile clamp would make this headline STEP UP 28px → 40px as
 *     the viewport crossed 760. Holding one clamp gives 50px → 28px → flat.
 *     (The step is a WRAPPER fault affecting every section that uses a bare
 *     .h2; it is reported there rather than papered over from here.)
 *   · The body's vertical pad states clamp(28px,4.6vw,52px) once, at base, and
 *     is likewise never restated.
 *   · Every fixed size in the 520 query is SMALLER than its base value
 *     (name 17.5→16.5, price 17→16, description 14→13.5, category 11.5→11,
 *     index 11→10.5, head paragraph 14.5→14), so nothing on this section can
 *     grow as the screen narrows.
 *
 * SPECIFICITY (R3). PageBN wraps its primitives in :where(), so a scoped rule
 * here at (0,2,0) beats the global at (0,1,1) — media queries included. So
 * never restate a property the wrapper tunes per breakpoint without restating it
 * at that breakpoint too (that is why .pcwm-note releases its own 34ch measure at
 * 760, where the wrapper's release reaches .lede and .h2 but not this class).
 * The owner's <em> inside the headline arrives through set:html AFTER scoping
 * and is unreachable from this block by design — the wrapper's global `.h2 em`
 * rule paints it rust, which is why nothing here needs :global().
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any;
}
const { content } = Astro.props;


const services = (content && content.services) || {};

const str = (v: any) => (typeof v === 'string' ? v.trim() : '');

// `services` is a wrapper OR a bare legacy array, so every scalar read has to
// tolerate both — on an array these are simply undefined, which is what the
// guards below expect.
const headline = str(services.headline);
const sub = str(services.sub);

type Row = { title: string; origin: string; notes: string; price: string };

const rawItems: any[] = Array.isArray(services.items)
  ? services.items
  : (Array.isArray(content?.services) ? content.services : []);

// All three vocabularies — see DATA SHAPES in the block above.
const items: Row[] = rawItems.map((it: any) => {
  if (typeof it === 'string') return { title: it.trim(), origin: '', notes: '', price: '' };
  if (!it || typeof it !== 'object') return { title: '', origin: '', notes: '', price: '' };
  return {
    title: str(it.title) || str(it.name),
    origin: str(it.tag),
    notes: str(it.desc) || str(it.description),
    price: str(it.price),
  };
});

// Drop the rows an owner added and never filled — carrying the ORIGINAL index
// (R6), so deleting row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3. Only the four
// fields this row DRAWS count; see THE ROW FILTER above.
const rows = items
  .map((r, i: number) => ({ ...r, i }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.title || r.origin || r.notes || r.price));

// A column exists only if some row fills it — see THE LEDGER'S COLUMNS above.
const hasName = rows.some((r) => Boolean(r.title));
const hasOrigin = rows.some((r) => Boolean(r.origin));
const hasNotes = rows.some((r) => Boolean(r.notes));
const hasPrice = rows.some((r) => Boolean(r.price));

// The design's five tracks, minus the ones nothing fills. The index track is
// unconditional because the numeral is drawn for every row.
const tracks = [
  'minmax(0,48px)',
  hasName ? 'minmax(0,1.5fr)' : '',
  hasOrigin ? 'minmax(0,1fr)' : '',
  hasNotes ? 'minmax(0,1.2fr)' : '',
  hasPrice ? 'minmax(0,90px)' : '',
].filter(Boolean).join(' ');

// …and the column each cell is pinned to, counted over the surviving tracks
// only. A cell whose column is absent is never rendered, so its 0 is unused.
let __col = 1;
const cName = hasName ? ++__col : 0;
const cOrigin = hasOrigin ? ++__col : 0;
const cNotes = hasNotes ? ++__col : 0;
const cPrice = hasPrice ? ++__col : 0;

// Custom properties ONLY. No colour, no size, no rule travels inline (R3).
const ledgerVars =
  `--pcwm-tracks:${tracks};--pcwm-c-name:${cName};--pcwm-c-origin:${cOrigin};` +
  `--pcwm-c-notes:${cNotes};--pcwm-c-price:${cPrice};`;

// R5: body content only. The headline is derived on every site, the sub on
// every site with a business name; neither can gate this section.
const hasSection = rows.length > 0;
---
{hasSection && (
  <section class="sec pcwm" id="menu">
    {/* The rail is the spine's index label — derived, decorative, never stored. */}
    <div class="rail" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rail-t">Menu</span></div>
    <div class="sec-body pcwm-body">
      {(headline || sub) && (
        <div class="pcwm-head reveal">
          {headline && <h2 class="h2 pcwm-h2" data-field="services.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
          {sub && <p class="pcwm-note" data-field="services.sub">{sub}</p>}
        </div>
      )}

      <ul class="pcwm-ledger reveal" role="list" style={ledgerVars}>
        {rows.map((r, k) => (
          <li class="pcwm-row">
            {/* DISPLAY position (R6), not the stored index: a printed ledger
                counts the lines it prints. */}
            <span class="pcwm-ref" aria-hidden="true">{String(k + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            {r.title && <span class="pcwm-name" data-field={`services.items.${r.i}.title`}>{r.title}</span>}
            {r.origin && <span class="pcwm-origin" data-field={`services.items.${r.i}.tag`}>{r.origin}</span>}
            {r.notes && <span class="pcwm-notes" data-field={`services.items.${r.i}.desc`}>{r.notes}</span>}
            {/* Printed exactly as typed — never converted, never totalled. */}
            {r.price && <span class="pcwm-price" data-field={`services.items.${r.i}.price`}>{r.price}</span>}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* One scoped block. Read the SPECIFICITY note in the frontmatter before adding
     to it. (Never write a literal end-of-stylesheet tag inside a comment here,
     not even as an example — this is a raw-text element and the parser would end
     the stylesheet on the spot, silently truncating the rest of the file.)

     `.pcwm` itself carries NO rule, on purpose and not by omission: the whole of
     this section's frame — the two-column spine, the rail hairline and the ink
     rule beneath — belongs to the wrapper's .sec / .rail primitives, and the
     class is here only so the section is nameable from a devtools inspection. */

  /* The design pads this body 52px 34px, where the wrapper's shared .sec-body
     tops out at 44px. The horizontal clamp already caps at the design's 34px, so
     only the vertical pad is restated — once, at base, with a positive slope, so
     it is monotonic by construction. */
  .pcwm-body { padding-top: clamp(28px, 4.6vw, 52px); padding-bottom: clamp(28px, 4.6vw, 52px); }

  /* ── THE SPLIT HEAD. h2 hard left, paragraph hard right, sharing a baseline
        at the bottom and wrapping to two lines when the pair runs out of room —
        which is exactly what the design's flex-wrap is for, and why the h2's
        measure is released below rather than capped. */
  .pcwm-head {
    display: flex; align-items: flex-end; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 26px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-bottom: 30px;
  }

  /* Face, weight, colour, tracking, uppercase and the rust <em> all stay with
     the wrapper's .h2. Only the size, the LEADING, the margin and the measure
     are this section's: the design gives this headline no measure cap and no
     bottom margin, because the head's own 30px does that job — and it states
     line-height 1, where the shared .h2 states .98, which is neither of the two
     figures the design uses (.96 on the hero h1, 1 on every section h2). At the
     50px cap the difference is 1px, but it is the design's number, all six other
     BN sections that resize an .h2 restate it beside the clamp, and a headline
     that leads differently from its neighbours is the drift the spine exists to
     prevent.
     Declared ONCE, with the size, and never restated in a query. */
  .pcwm-h2 {
    margin: 0; max-width: none; min-width: 0;
    font-size: clamp(28px, 3.6vw, 50px);
    line-height: 1;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* The design's head paragraph is 14.5px/1.7 over 34ch in --ink-2 — smaller,
     tighter and darker than the wrapper's .lede, which is sized for the hero, so
     it is its own rule rather than a .lede override. Qualified by its class so
     it beats the wrapper's (0,1,1) paragraph reset and its 1em bottom margin. */
  .pcwm-note {
    margin: 0; max-width: 34ch; min-width: 0;
    font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--ink-2);
    text-transform: none; letter-spacing: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── THE LEDGER. It opens on the FULL-INK rule (--rule-strong, the same weight
        as the rule between sections) and closes each entry on the grey hairline;
        those two rules are the only borders in this section. role="list" on the
        element keeps the list semantics that list-style:none strips. */
  .pcwm-ledger {
    list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  }

  /* Tracks come from the frontmatter (THE LEDGER'S COLUMNS) so a column nobody
     filled costs no width. */
  .pcwm-row {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: var(--pcwm-tracks);
    gap: 20px; padding: 17px 0; align-items: baseline;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); min-width: 0;
  }

  /* Every cell is pinned EXPLICITLY: auto-placement would slide a description
     into the category's column on any row that omitted one. */

  /* Line-height 1.25 on the four cells the design leaves unset: the design's own
     page declares none, so they render at the browser normal there, while this
     wrapper sets body line-height 1.62 — inheriting it would set a ledger in
     body-copy leading. The description keeps the 1.6 the design states. */
  .pcwm-ref {
    grid-column: 1; min-width: 0;
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11px; line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .pcwm-name {
    grid-column: var(--pcwm-c-name); min-width: 0;
    font-weight: 700; font-size: 17.5px; line-height: 1.25; letter-spacing: -.01em;
    color: var(--ink); overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .pcwm-origin {
    grid-column: var(--pcwm-c-origin); min-width: 0;
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11.5px; line-height: 1.25;
    letter-spacing: .04em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .pcwm-notes {
    grid-column: var(--pcwm-c-notes); min-width: 0;
    font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-2);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .pcwm-price {
